  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 PM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows a rather potent upper level trough grazing \nLake Superior as it lifts into northern Ontario this afternoon, with \nan associated 1005 mb surface low analyzed over NE Ontario and \ntracking towards James Bay. A strong low level jet at the base of \nthe trough is crossing the UP this afternoon, helping to produce \ngusty west-southwest winds with multiple gusts of 40-45 mph from the \nKeweenaw and east along the shoreline. Gusts of 25-35 mph have been \ncommon across the rest of the UP today. Temperatures range from the \n50s in the west to the low 60s across the central and eastern UP as \nof 2 PM, putting multiple daily records in jeopardy. The warm and \nwindy conditions coupled with humidity values in the 30s are also \nleading to limited fire weather concerns across the area this \nafternoon.\n\nWinds will diminish through this evening and overnight as the low \ncontinues to track off to the northeast and the surface pressure \ngradient slackens, allowing temperatures to drop back down into the \n30s for most locations tonight. Transient upper level ridging \ncrosses the area on Friday, making for another unseasonably warm day \nwith highs in the 50s to low 60s. However, the period of relatively \ncalm conditions will be short lived as a deep upper trough \napproaches from the western CONUS and another surface low lifts out \nof the Rockies, following a very similar track to today's low as it \npasses north of Lake Superior into northern Ontario by Saturday \nmorning. This will spur another round of gusty winds beginning \nFriday afternoon, though the direction will be more southerly with \nthis next system. Although winds are not as expected to be as strong \nas today's system, gusts to 15-25 mph will be common for most areas \nof the UP, with 35+ mph gusts possible (50-70% chance) along the \ndownslope areas near Lake Superior and the Keweenaw.\n\nSaturday will bring one more day of well above normal temps with \nhighs in the 50s, but a cold front moving into the UP Saturday \nevening will bring a pattern change back to cooler and wetter \nconditions as rain spreads back into the western UP late in the day. \nHow much rain the UP receives will be determined by whether or not \nand where a surface wave is able to develop along the front, which \nis still a source of disagreement amongst the models. The NBM \ncontinues to highlight a roughly 20-40% chance for an inch or more \nof rainfall through Sunday evening, with the best chances for higher \namounts over the central UP. Temperatures crash as the upper trough \nmoves overhead Sunday night into Monday, bringing a round of lake \neffect snow in the north to northwest winds belts through Monday. \nAmounts still look to be rather limited with the probability for \neven 2 inches of accumulation currently sitting at around 10-20% \nover the western half through Monday evening. Thus, the most likely \nscenario continues to be for minor accumulations in the higher \nterrain areas and little to no accumulation closer to the lakeshore. \nStrong surface high pressure then builds in from Tuesday into the \nmiddle of next week, favoring a return to drier weather and more \nnormal temperatures for mid/late November with highs in the 30s and \nlows in the teens and 20s.",
